L0000:	db	'bye'
	db	00
L0004:	db	'job'
	db	00
L0008:	db	'name'
	db	00
L000D:	db	'Fellowship'
	db	00
L0018:	db	'You see a pleasant-looking young peasant woman.'
	db	00
L0048:	db	'"Greetings to thee, '
	db	00
L005D:	db	'," says Diane.'
	db	00
L006C:	db	'name'
	db	00
L0071:	db	'"My name is Diane."'
	db	00
L0085:	db	'name'
	db	00
L008A:	db	'job'
	db	00
L008E:	db	'"My job is to oversee the stables here in Britain and sell t'
	db	'hee a horse and carriage if thou dost want one."'
	db	00
L00FB:	db	'carriage'
	db	00
L0104:	db	'Britain'
	db	00
L010C:	db	'stables'
	db	00
L0114:	db	'stables'
	db	00
L011C:	db	'"Here thou wilt find a selection of the finest horses produc'
	db	'ed by Lord British\'s personal horse breeder. If thou dost w'
	db	'ish to buy one, I am sure we can come to some sort of arrang'
	db	'ement. They come with a carriage, of course."'
	db	00
L01FD:	db	'stables'
	db	00
L0205:	db	'Britain'
	db	00
L020D:	db	'"Britain is such a grand city, but it can be somewhat discon'
	db	'certing if thou dost not know anyone. Fortunately, I know ma'
	db	'ny people here."'
	db	00
L0296:	db	'Britain'
	db	00
L029E:	db	'people'
	db	00
L02A5:	db	'carriage'
	db	00
L02AE:	db	'"The horse and carriage combination sells for 120 gold. Thou'
	db	' shalt find it in a small shelter just south of the stables,'
	db	' across the street. Dost thou want a title?"'
	db	00
L0353:	db	'"Very good. Here is thy title."'
	db	00
L0373:	db	'"Oh, my. Thine hands are too full to take the title!"'
	db	00
L03A9:	db	'"Oh. Thou dost not have enough gold to buy the title."'
	db	00
L03E0:	db	'"Some other time, then."'
	db	00
L03F9:	db	'carriage'
	db	00
L0402:	db	'people'
	db	00
L0409:	db	'"I have many friends in Britannia. Among them are Greg, Jame'
	db	's, Brownie and Mack."'
	db	00
L045B:	db	'people'
	db	00
L0462:	db	'Mack'
	db	00
L0467:	db	'Brownie'
	db	00
L046F:	db	'James'
	db	00
L0475:	db	'Greg'
	db	00
L047A:	db	'Greg'
	db	00
L047F:	db	'"Greg runs a shop that sells provisions. If thou art plannin'
	db	'g any sort of expedition he is just the person to see. He se'
	db	'ems to be lucky. Perhaps some of it will rub off on thee."'
	db	00
L0532:	db	'Greg'
	db	00
L0537:	db	'James'
	db	00
L053D:	db	'"James, who runs an inn not far from here, wanted a life of '
	db	'adventure. His family wanted him to run the inn after his fa'
	db	'ther died and he has been discontent ever since. Still, I th'
	db	'ink he fancies Cynthia who works at the Mint."'
	db	00
L0620:	db	'James'
	db	00
L0626:	db	'Brownie'
	db	00
L062E:	db	'"Brownie is a decent and honest man who would have made a mu'
	db	'ch better mayor than Patterson if thou dost want mine opinio'
	db	'n. He uses our horses to plow his fields in the spring."'
	db	00
L06DF:	db	'Brownie'
	db	00
L06E7:	db	'Mack'
	db	00
L06EC:	db	'"One word of warning about Mack. Do not let him start talkin'
	db	'g about the sky. Other than that he is perfectly fine, I can'
	db	' assure thee."'
	db	00
L0773:	db	'Mack'
	db	00
L0778:	db	'Fellowship'
	db	00
L0783:	db	'Diane notices your Fellowship medallion. "It is odd. If thou'
	db	' dost not mind me saying so, thou dost not seem like a Fello'
	db	'wship member. There is something about thee. I cannot place '
	db	'it."'
	db	00
L083C:	db	'bye'
	db	00
L0840:	db	'"Good day to thee, '
	db	00
L0854:	db	'."*'
	db	00
