	; Function at file offset 000CEC1DH
	.funcnumber  04ADH
	.data
	.msize       1828H
	.dsize       1442H
L0000:	db	'bye'
	db	00
L0004:	db	'job'
	db	00
L0008:	db	'name'
	db	00
L000D:	db	'You see an old woman who gives you a smile of grandmotherly '
	db	'sweetness. You can see immediately that her vision is poor.'
	db	00
L0085:	db	'"Why, hello again, '
	db	00
L0099:	db	'. It is so good to see thee!" says Beverlea.'
	db	00
L00C6:	db	'name'
	db	00
L00CB:	db	'"My name is Beverlea."'
	db	00
L00E2:	db	'name'
	db	00
L00E7:	db	'job'
	db	00
L00EB:	db	'"Why, I run the House of Items here in Paws."'
	db	00
L0119:	db	'Paws'
	db	00
L011E:	db	'House of Items'
	db	00
L012D:	db	'House of Items'
	db	00
L013C:	db	'"It is a shop that sells antiques and previously used items.'
	db	' Running this shop allows me to remain youthful and active. '
	db	'It is heartening to sell things to the poor people of this t'
	db	'own that they might not otherwise be able to\tafford."'
	db	00
L0226:	db	'House of Items'
	db	00
L0235:	db	'buy'
	db	00
L0239:	db	'Paws'
	db	00
L023E:	db	'"Here in Paws the people have very little money, but it matt'
	db	'ers not, because they care for each other."'
	db	00
L02A6:	db	'Paws'
	db	00
L02AB:	db	'buy'
	db	00
L02AF:	db	'"There are many rare and fine things to be bought here in my'
	db	' shop. Bargains to be had nowhere else in all of Britannia."'
	db	00
L0328:	db	'many fine things'
	db	00
L0339:	db	'"My store is now closed. I am usually open in the afternoons'
	db	'."'
	db	00
L0378:	db	'buy'
	db	00
L037C:	db	'many fine things'
	db	00
L038D:	db	'"Let me see... There is a cradle for sale. A rocking horse. '
	db	'A bell. An hourglass. A spittoon. A lute. A sextant... Since'
	db	' I am moving a bit more slowly these days, I let my customer'
	db	's help themselves and take what they have bought. Providing '
	db	'they pay me first, of course. I do trust folks to pay me the'
	db	' correct amount. I am nearly blind, I am afraid."'
	db	00
L04EB:	db	'many fine things'
	db	00
L04FC:	db	'sextant'
	db	00
L0504:	db	'lute'
	db	00
L0509:	db	'spittoon'
	db	00
L0512:	db	'hourglass'
	db	00
L051C:	db	'bell'
	db	00
L0521:	db	'rocking horse'
	db	00
L052F:	db	'cradle'
	db	00
L0536:	db	'cradle'
	db	00
L053D:	db	'"That old cradle was the cradle used to rock Gorn the Barbar'
	db	'ian to sleep at night when he was just a baby. Thou canst se'
	db	'e that there is a crack in its side proving that even as a c'
	db	'hild Gorn was a strong little tyke. I can let thee take it f'
	db	'or ten gold coins. Dost thou wish to buy the cradle?"'
	db	00
L0663:	db	'"Thou mayest take it then. I hope that thou dost enjoy it."'
	db	00
L069F:	db	'"Thou dost not have enough gold!"'
	db	00
L06C1:	db	'A sour look flashes across Beverlea\'s face. "Very well. Per'
	db	'haps I may interest thee in something else."'
	db	00
L0729:	db	'cradle'
	db	00
L0730:	db	'rocking horse'
	db	00
L073E:	db	'"This is the rocking horse that once belonged to a little gi'
	db	'rl from Britain named Diane. She grew to be one of the fines'
	db	't equestriennes to ever sit upon a horse. I could let thee h'
	db	'ave this rare and unusual piece for twelve gold. Dost thou w'
	db	'ish to buy it?"'
	db	00
L083E:	db	'"Then thou art free to take the rocking horse. It is thine!"'
	db	00
L087B:	db	'"Thou dost not have enough money to pay for it!"'
	db	00
L08AC:	db	'Beverlea rolls her eyes. "In a browsing mood, are we? Take t'
	db	'hy time."'
	db	00
L08F2:	db	'rocking horse'
	db	00
L0900:	db	'bell'
	db	00
L0905:	db	'"That bell came from the High Court of Justice in Yew. It wa'
	db	's rung to announce that court was in session. I can sell the'
	db	'e that interesting conversation piece for six gold coins.\tD'
	db	'ost thou wish to buy it?"'
	db	00
L09D2:	db	'"Thou mayest take thy bell. Use it in good health!"'
	db	00
L0A06:	db	'"Thou dost not have enough money to buy it!"'
	db	00
L0A33:	db	'"Not in the mood for a bell today?" She wrinkles her lips in'
	db	' the best attempt at a smile. "Perhaps something else will s'
	db	'trike thy fancy."'
	db	00
L0ABD:	db	'bell'
	db	00
L0AC2:	db	'hourglass'
	db	00
L0ACC:	db	'"I also have an antique hourglass. It was sold to me by this'
	db	' old man who was so daft that he could not recall how to use'
	db	' it! I will sell it to thee for five gold pieces. Dost thou '
	db	'wish to buy it?"'
	db	00
L0B91:	db	'"I thank thee. Thou mayest take thy glass."'
	db	00
L0BBD:	db	'"Thou dost not have enough money."'
	db	00
L0BE0:	db	'"Thou art not interested in the hourglass?" She sighs, "Very'
	db	' well. Look around. I have all the time in the world." You c'
	db	'atch just a hint of sarcasm in her voice.'
	db	00
L0C82:	db	'"Curse mine old head and failing memory! Has the hourglass a'
	db	'lready been sold?! No! It must have been stolen! One of the '
	db	'few dishonest people in this town must have taken it!"'
	db	00
L0D31:	db	'hourglass'
	db	00
L0D3B:	db	'spittoon'
	db	00
L0D44:	db	'"I also have an old spittoon. It was once used by... a great'
	db	' many people. Thou mayest have it for a gold piece. Take it!'
	db	' Please!"'
	db	00
L0DC6:	db	'"I thank thee! Now thou canst go and please do not forget to'
	db	' take it with thee when thou dost leave!"'
	db	00
L0E2C:	db	'"Thou dost not even have a single gold piece! Nor the decenc'
	db	'y to inform me that I was wasting my time in talking with th'
	db	'ee."'
	db	00
L0EA9:	db	'"I have many more things in my shop. Many things of high qua'
	db	'lity and great value as well. Please keep looking."'
	db	00
L0F19:	db	'spittoon'
	db	00
L0F22:	db	'lute'
	db	00
L0F27:	db	'"I have a lute for sale that once belonged to a travelling b'
	db	'ard who lost it in a game of dice. I am asking twenty gold c'
	db	'oins for it. A song! Wouldst thou like to buy it?"'
	db	00
L0FD2:	db	'"I thank thee, kind customer. Thou mayest take thy lute. I c'
	db	'an see that thou art a true artisan with an appreciation for'
	db	' quality."'
	db	00
L1055:	db	'"Thou canst not afford the lute!"'
	db	00
L1077:	db	'"Very well, then. Do keep looking. After all, that is why my'
	db	' shop is here." You thought you could also hear Beverlea mut'
	db	'ter to herself for a few moments after saying this, but you '
	db	'are not entirely certain.'
	db	00
L1145:	db	'lute'
	db	00
L114A:	db	'sextant'
	db	00
L1152:	db	'"I have a sextant that was sold by the world-famous shipwrig'
	db	'ht Owen of Minoc. They are going to be building a monument t'
	db	'o him, I understand. Anyway, the sailor who sold it to me ha'
	db	'd just suffered some harrowing experience out on the waters.'
	db	' He said when he sold it to me that he was going to retire. '
	db	'He obviously did not realize the value of this item. But I c'
	db	'an let thee have it for twenty gold pieces. Dost thou wish t'
	db	'o buy it?"'
	db	00
L1301:	db	'"Thou mayest take thy sextant! And may thou always have smoo'
	db	'th sailing!"'
	db	00
L134A:	db	'"Thou dost not have enough money!"'
	db	00
L136D:	db	'"I am certain I have something that will interest thee. Brow'
	db	'se to thine heart\'s content."'
	db	00
L13C7:	db	'sextant'
	db	00
L13CF:	db	'bye'
	db	00
