L4A19:	db	'~~ ~~VARGAZ\'S STORIES OF LEGEND~~Reasons why one should nev'
	db	'er build doors facing north or west.*'
	db	00
L4A7A:	db	'     Many centuries ago, the prophet, Father Antos, foretold'
	db	' the coming of a plaque of Locust that would arrive from the'
	db	' lands to the north.  He predicted that their source area wa'
	db	's so important that it would indicate who would survive the '
	db	'attack, and who would not. He predicted that anyone living i'
	db	'n a house having a door with a northern exposure would peris'
	db	'h under the onslaught. Two days later, the locusts came, and'
	db	' in the aftermath, it was discoverd that only the houses wit'
	db	'h doors on the north wall were destroyed.~~'
	db	00
L4C86:	db	'     ...The naturalist, Ergan, incorporating Algarth\'s disc'
	db	'overy that the sun rises from the east, theorized that the n'
	db	'ightly path of many dark-dwelling nasties could be traced. A'
	db	's the sun moves slowly to the west, Ergan contends, shadows '
	db	'increase in the east, forcing monsters to move that directio'
	db	'n to stay out of the sun. Therefore, as denizens of the dark'
	db	' travel from the west, they are more likely to invade househ'
	db	'olds with doors that directly cross their paths... '
	db	00
L4E5D:	db	'~~ ~~ONE HUNDRED AND ONE WAYS TO CHEAT AT NIM~~ ~~by Dr. Cat'
	db	'*'
	db	00
L4E9B:	db	'     Within the pages of this tome are all the many ways to '
	db	'earn supplementary gold by gambling at Nim. Written by the o'
	db	'riginator of the game, himself, this book covers in depth su'
	db	'ch strategies as, "claw first, question later" and "there ar'
	db	'e no ways to skin a cat."'
	db	00
L4FA5:	db	'~~ ~~PLAY DIRECTING: ANALYSIS, COMMUNICATION AND STYLE~~ ~~b'
	db	'y Francis Hodge*'
	db	00
L4FF2:	db	'     Within the pages of this highly respected textbook one '
	db	'can find the highly touted, and sometimes controversial, met'
	db	'hods of staging a play. Written by an eminent Professor Emer'
	db	'itus from a university in a distant land, this book is consi'
	db	'dered by most thespians as the definitive source book on dir'
	db	'ecting.'
	db	00
L5126:	db	'~~ ~~ON ACTING~~ ~~by Laurence Olivier*'
	db	00
L514E:	db	'     Within the pages of this tome are the theories and phil'
	db	'osophies of acting, as well as personal anecdotes, written b'
	db	'y a noted thespian of a distant land. Apparently this book w'
	db	'as one of the many brought to Britannia by Lord British.'
	db	00
L523B:	db	'~~ ~~THOU ART WHAT THEE EATS~~ ~~by Fordras*'
	db	00
L5268:	db	'     Within these pages thou wilt find the comparitive analy'
	db	'sis of many of things we, humans, place in our bodies in the'
	db	' name of food. I will attempt to provide for thee informatio'
	db	'n on what constitutes "good" food and what constitutes "bad,'
	db	'" and will display the information by mentioning each type f'
	db	'rom best to worst, first in terms of nutritional value and s'
	db	'econd by taste.*'
	db	00
L53E1:	db	'     A large chop of fine meat, including mutton, fowl, ham,'
	db	' or ribs, is by far the most nourishing.  This does not incl'
	db	'ude other forms of beef, however, for they are usually serve'
	db	'd in smaller portions. Pork and sausage are also lower on th'
	db	'e proverbial "scale," for they are not quite as filling. In '
	db	'place of meat, I would recommend flounder, cheese, or potato'
	db	'es, for they are also quite good for thee.~     In some inst'
	db	'ances, trout, fish and chips, and some breads will pass for '
	db	'a meal. An egg and most any other fruit and vegetable, inclu'
	db	'ding: an apple, a banana, a carrot, a pumpkin, a bunch of gr'
	db	'apes, and various cakes, will suffice in a pinch. However, d'
	db	'espite its delectible taste and extravagant price, Silverlea'
	db	'f meals have absolutely no value on this chart at all. The m'
	db	'oral is, my friend, never pass up meats when thou hast the c'
	db	'hance to dine upon them!*'
	db	00
L5743:	db	'     Obviously, not everything that tastes good is nourishin'
	db	'g. At the top of this list, I must put down Silverleaf. The '
	db	'taste is absolutely exquisite! Short of that, I recommend ro'
	db	'ast mutton with a lovely Minoxian glaze sauce. Add a potato '
	db	'as a side course, with the whole meal preceeded by a few raw'
	db	' vegetables, and thou truly hast a wonderful feast!~     For'
	db	' a second course, I would suggest...'
	db	00
L58D0:	db	'~~ ~~MAN VERSUS FISH: THE ULTIMATE CONFLICT~~ ~~by Aquastyr*'
	db	00
L590D:	db	'     Thou might consider fishing to be an activity designed '
	db	'simply to feed thy familty. Daily thou takest thy fishing po'
	db	'le and trod to the lake in hopes of catching thy dinner. How'
	db	'ever, say I, fishing is much more!~'
	db	00
L59E5:	db	'     A fisherman is much like a knight, off to conquer the t'
	db	'errible dragon. Certainly, the terrain is familiar, just as '
	db	'the knight\'s homeland is to him, but the contest is where t'
	db	'he real comparison lies. While a knight carries with him a g'
	db	'reat and powerful sword, and fisherman must arm himself with'
	db	' a sturdy pole.~'
	db	00
L5B21:	db	'     Of course, another important weapon for the fisherman i'
	db	's his mind. He will need a keen sense of observation to aid '
	db	'him in his struggle against the slippery opponents. The firs'
	db	't thing he must do is search for a body of water where the f'
	db	'ish are so common that they are forced to jump above the wat'
	db	'er simply to have room for the others. A lake or pond filled'
	db	' with fish will provide the wise fisherman a better opportun'
	db	'ity for an encounter.~'
	db	00
L5CDC:	db	'     And how does a fisherman catch his prey. Not by sitting'
	db	' idly by and waiting for them to jump into his pail! For onc'
	db	'e the creature grabs hold of the bait, the fisherman must fi'
	db	'ght and thrash his way, as if attacking the very beast, itse'
	db	'lf. Minutes seem to turn to hours as the warrior battles wit'
	db	'h his fish, until finally, all energy spent from both sides,'
	db	' the fish is dragged unwillingly into the waiting bucket.~'
	db	00
L5E7F:	db	'     And then, the process must begin anew...'
	db	00
L5EAD:	db	'~~ ~~KNIGHT\'S BRIDGE IN A NUTSHELL~~ ~~by Nicodemus*'
	db	00
L5EE2:	db	'     This is a board game for two players.  Each player begi'
	db	'ns with three pieces. The object is to move thy three pieces'
	db	' in concert in order to force the treasure from the center o'
	db	'f the board to thine own side. All the while, thine opponent'
	db	' shall be attempting to do the same.~~'
	db	00
L5FF9:	db	'Each turn consists of a player either "moving" or "pushing" '
	db	'a piece followed by a similar action by the opposing player.'
	db	'~~'
	db	00
L6074:	db	'A legal "move" consists of placing one\'s piece in any adjac'
	db	'ent, unoccupied black or white square. A legal "push" is per'
	db	'formed when a player forces a piece, either an opponent\'s o'
	db	'r the treasure, one space directly away from his piece.~~'
	db	00
L6160:	db	'The exception to the latter rule, making a "push" illegal, o'
	db	'ccurs when a player wishes to "push" a piece into the space '
	db	'from which it just left in the preceding turn.~~'
	db	00
L6209:	db	'If any piece, excluding the treasure, is "pushed" onto a blu'
	db	'e square or off the board, that piece is considered lost, an'
	db	'd is removed from play. The treasure may not be "pushed" off'
	db	' the board.~~'
	db	00
L62CB:	db	'To win the game, a player must "push" the treasure to the ba'
	db	'ck row of squares on his side of the board.'
	db	00
L6333:	db	'~~ ~~MEMPTO RAYS~~A qualitative study in metaparaphilosophic'
	db	'al radiation~~ ~~by Mempto*'
	db	00
L638B:	db	'     Despite Felcrodan\'s theory of 0335, there are, indeed,'
	db	' rays of energy that constantly bombard Britannia. In fact, '
	db	'these very same rays permeate of all the known space between'
	db	' Britannia and the stars. Recent experiments have proven my '
	db	'theory that these rays, known hereafter as `Mempto Rays,\' a'
	db	're lethal to all non-living matter. In fact, Mempto rays hav'
	db	'e demonstrated their ability numerous times, once killing an'
	db	' entire boulder in a matter of a few hours. It is my recomme'
	db	'ndation...'
	db	00
L6574:	db	'~~ ~~THE BOOK OF CIRCLES~~ ~~Translated from Gargish to Brit'
	db	'annian by Jillian*'
	db	00
L65C3:	db	'     All begins with the three principles: Control, Passion '
	db	'and Diligence.~'
	db	00
L660F:	db	'     From Control springs Direction. From Passion springs Fe'
	db	'eling. From Diligence springs Persistence.~'
	db	00
L6677:	db	'     But these three virtues are no more important than the '
	db	'other five: Control combines with Passion to give Balance. P'
	db	'assion combines with Diligence to yield Achievement. And Dil'
	db	'igence joins with Control to provide Precision.~'
	db	00
L675C:	db	'     The absence of Control, Passion, and Diligence is Chaos'
	db	'. Thus absence of the principles points toward the seventh v'
	db	'irture, Order.~'
	db	00
L67E4:	db	'     The three principles unify to form Singularity. This is'
	db	' the eight virtue, but it is also the first, because within '
	db	'Singularity can be found all the principles, and thus all th'
	db	'e virtues.~'
	db	00
L68A4:	db	'     A circle has no end. It continues forever, with all par'
	db	'ts equally\timportant to the success of the whole.~'
	db	00
L6913:	db	'     Our society is the same. It, too, continues forever, wi'
	db	'th all members (and all virtues) equeal parts of the unified'
	db	' whole.'
	db	00
L6993:	db	'~~ ~~CHICKEN RAISING~~ ~~by Daheness Gon*'
	db	00
L69BD:	db	'     While handling chickens is a relatively easy task, as i'
	db	's collecting eggs, there are several pieces of information t'
	db	'hat will be of much use to the novice owner. For example, qu'
	db	'ite a number of hens have soft, delect fluff on their unders'
	db	'ide which they developed as chicks, but never outgrew. As a '
	db	'result, the coarse straw of the nest tends to irritate their'
	db	' hide. As a result, many hens will bury their eggs beneath a'
	db	' thin layer of straw. This provides warmth that the egg is m'
	db	'issing because the mother will not fully set herself down up'
	db	'on the egg.~'
	db	00
L6BE6:	db	'     In addition, most hens do not lay their eggs at the sam'
	db	'e time as their sister hens. A hen must rest at the minimum '
	db	'three full hours before she will capable of producing anothe'
	db	'r. And do not be concerned if more time is necessary for thy'
	db	' hens to create more of their offspring.'
	db	00
L6CFF:	db	'~~ ~~THE ART OF FIELD DRESSING~~ ~~by Creston~Forward by Lad'
	db	'y Leigh*'
	db	00
L6D44:	db	'     Though I personally advocate the use of healing herbs a'
	db	'nd more sterile bandaging environments, the steps told in th'
	db	'is treatise will suffice until better healing care becomes a'
	db	'vailable. Follow  Creston\'s steps carefully, and thou wilt '
	db	'have no trouble forming a reliable, if not perfect, way to p'
	db	'revent serious blood-loss. Remember, however, that there is '
	db	'no care as good as clean sheets, a sturdy bed, and proper at'
	db	'tention!~~'
	db	00
L6EF2:	db	'-- Lady Leigh of Serpent\'s Hold*'
	db	00
L6F13:	db	'     As long as one has shears, cloth, and a conscious mind,'
	db	' thou wilt have little fear of bleeding to death on the corp'
	db	'se-littered fields of battle. Simply cut the cloth into stri'
	db	'ps and bandages. Quickly placing the center of the bandage o'
	db	'ver the wound, wrap the strip around and around the bleeding'
	db	' area. As the cloth begins to soak up the blood, it will com'
	db	'bine with the natural healing aspects of the body to staunch'
	db	' the flow of blood and to quicken the healing process. In no'
	db	' time at all, thou wilt be ready to travel again (though I a'
	db	'dvise this travel to be to the house of a competent healer!)'
	db	'.'
	db	00
L716D:	db	'~~ ~~WATER ON THE CAT~~ ~~by Mowze*'
	db	00
L7191:	db	'     (This text is a humorous diatribe, and should be taken '
	db	'as nothing more than such. Any of the following activities s'
	db	'hould only be performed when water is abundant enough to be '
	db	'in surplus. Droughts are serious business, and this work is '
	db	'in no way designed to indicate otherwise.~     -- The editor'
	db	')~'
	db	00
L72C0:	db	'     The using of a bucket to draw water from a well takes n'
	db	'othing more than common sense, however UPON what to use the '
	db	'water is often a more difficult question to answer. Cats are'
	db	', of course, my personal favorite objects of such torture. B'
	db	'ut, thy friends will usually do quite nicely, providing they'
	db	' have no form of retaliation and are not quick to anger.~   '
	db	'  Children are a little less choice, though considerably mor'
	db	'e deserving, for their parents DO often anger quickly when t'
	db	'heir offspring is threatened.'
	db	00
L74BE:	db	'~~ ~~A SHORT TREATISE ON BRITANNIAN SOCIETY~~ ~~by Clayton*'
	db	00
L74FA:	db	'     The hierarchy of the peoples of Britannia follows a tra'
	db	'ditional order. Though little here is meant to be law, the e'
	db	'ntire content was compiled from observartiions made througho'
	db	'ut the land.~'
	db	00
L75BC:	db	'     All citizens of Britannia, obviously, owe alleigance to'
	db	' the just and noble Lord British. The next in line to receiv'
	db	'e our respect are the members of the Great Council, which co'
	db	'mprises one citizen from each town or city in Britannia.~'
	db	00
L76AA:	db	'     Members of the Great Council are followed by the winged'
	db	' gargoyles, for they are highly educated and well-versed in '
	db	'all matters of life. Many a winged friend has supplied words'
	db	' of wisdom to a traveler venturing through Terfin.~'
	db	00
L7792:	db	'     Just below the winged gargoyles follow the wonderous ma'
	db	'sses of humanity, without whom the economic and governmental'
	db	' aspects would collapse. These, of course, are followed by t'
	db	'he less worldly, but quite, quite strong wingless gargoyles.'
	db	'~'
	db	00
L7884:	db	'     Much of the impact upon our economic system has come fr'
	db	'om...'
	db	00
L78C6:	db	'~~ ~~A BAKER\'S HANDBOOK~~ ~~by Settlar*'
	db	00
L78EE:	db	'     The fundamentals for baking all come from knowledge of '
	db	'how to perform one simple task: baking bread. If thou art ab'
	db	'le to do that, thou canst bake anything, from meat pies to t'
	db	'arts for thy guests.~'
	db	00
L79B8:	db	'     It is important to set the flour first upon the table. '
	db	'Many bakers prefer to manipulate the dough on the hearth its'
	db	'elf, but I prefer preheating the hearth (it makes for more t'
	db	'horough baking), so that is not possible.~'
	db	00
L7A97:	db	'     To the four, add water and mix until the two are well c'
	db	'ombined. The dough should be damp enough to be kneaded easil'
	db	'y but is should not be runny. Now is the time to place the d'
	db	'ough on the hearth and bake it. After but a few minutes, tho'
	db	'u wilt have quite tasty bread to eat.~'
	db	00
L7BAE:	db	'     Now, the next item will prove a true treat for thy love'
	db	'd ones...'
	db	00
L7BF4:	db	'~~ Logbook~~ Astelleron*'
	db	00
L7C0D:	db	'... I have grown lonely here on the island. Despite my golem'
	db	's, I have no one with whom I can converse, no one with any p'
	db	'ersonality. Even the animals spend less time here than on th'
	db	'e main island.~Each day I look upon the horizon for a sign o'
	db	'f someone. I have no fear of strangers, for either I will me'
	db	'et the Avatar, or the golems I created to protect the Shrine'
	db	's will fend off hostile visitors...~'
	db	00
L7D9A:	db	'... I am exhilirated! Today, while on the main island, I hap'
	db	'pened by a tree. While this is not inherently odd, I noticed'
	db	' that the tree seemed to grow not out of the ground, but fro'
	db	'm a large rock. Equally unusual was the five stones surround'
	db	'ing it, each located the same distance from each other and f'
	db	'rom the center stone. They looked much like they could repre'
	db	'sent vertices of a five-pointed star.~And then I realized to'
	db	' what I was a witness: the legendary Stone of Castambre. Eve'
	db	'n had I not noticed the Tree of Life springing forth from th'
	db	'e boulder, the Pentacle of rocks gave all away...'
	db	00
L7FE8:	db	'... The first test was a success. I used a pick to chip away'
	db	' a bit of the stone. I was startled at first by the bleeding'
	db	', but as I heard not a whit of any sound indicating pain, I '
	db	'continued. I am about to confer with the book to determine m'
	db	'y next action...'
	db	00
L80E9:	db	'... I am afraid I will have little time to continue this jou'
	db	'rnal for the moment. I realize that a true scientist would r'
	db	'ecord daily with the utmost accuracy what he has done and wi'
	db	'tnessed, but the amount of work each day requires leaves me '
	db	'long past the point of exhaustion...'
	db	00
L81FE:	db	'... I have done it! My newest two golems can actually speak!'
	db	' And they offer original comments, not mere echoes of my own'
	db	' thought. The instructions in the book are correct. Bollux, '
	db	'my first attempt, succeeded, but my inexperience left him a '
	db	'little less intelligent than I would have preferred. However'
	db	', his "brother," Adjhar, benefited from my mistake with the '
	db	'other, and has full speech capabilities. As I sit now, writi'
	db	'ng this, I can hear them discussing weather! I must go now a'
	db	'nd talk with them. Oddly enough, the sky no longer seems ove'
	db	'rcast...'
	db	00
L8423:	db	'~~ ~~GOLEMS: FROM CLAY TO STONE~~ ~~by Castadon*'
	db	00
L8454:	db	'... Stone golems can be created from any hard rock. However,'
	db	' it is important to note that the enchantments require they '
	db	'be anthropomorphic\tin shape. Any other construct, or an inc'
	db	'omplete one, will not be able to hold the creature together '
	db	'or permit locomotion.~'
	db	00
L855A:	db	'     Once the sufficient amount of stone has been gathered a'
	db	'nd placed roughly in the shape of a man, thou must cast the '
	db	'Vas Rel Ailem\tspell (see appendix QQ for spell description)'
	db	' to form the rock into a person. Needless to record, perhaps'
	db	', the creature will barely resemble anything human, but will'
	db	' be able to function similarly.~'
	db	00
L86A6:	db	'     The next enchantment is Kal Mani (appendix QQ). This wi'
	db	'll "breathe life" into the newly created golems, or rather, '
	db	'breathe animation into them. Once created, each golem will h'
	db	'ave enough rudimentary intelligence to obey and respond to t'
	db	'hree single-word commands, or one extensive request of any l'
	db	'ength.*'
	db	00
L87DA:	db	'*'
	db	00
L87DC:	db	'     Appendix K~     The Stone of Castambre'
	db	00
L8808:	db	'~~     This mythological rock has legendary powers that perm'
	db	'its one to shape and enchant stone -- and only stone -- to c'
	db	'reate golems by following only a single, short ritual instea'
	db	'd of the lengthy and time-consuming process described in pre'
	db	'vious chapters. Though the Stone\'s existence has never been'
	db	' confirmed, there are also other purported powers that could'
	db	' make a risky investigation quite worth while. For additiona'
	db	'l information, see "The Stone of Castambre," by MacCuth.'
	db	00
L89E4:	db	'~~ ~~THE STONE OF CASTAMBRE~~ ~~by MacCuth*'
	db	00
L8A10:	db	'     Legendary rock? Perhaps. Powerful relic? Definitely.~Th'
	db	'e Stone Of Castambre -- named for the mage who is rumored to'
	db	' have enchanted and placed it -- is said to be located on th'
	db	'e Isle of Fire, also the location of the Shrines of the Thre'
	db	'e Principles. Of course, since knowledge of the Isle has lon'
	db	'g since disappeared, knowledge of the infamous Stone is equa'
	db	'lly mysterious. However, through research and study of Casta'
	db	'mbre\'s diary I have brought to light a few clues to the Sto'
	db	'ne\'s whereabouts.*'
	db	00
L8C02:	db	'     The major purpose of the Stone\'s power is to animate i'
	db	'nanimate objects: statues, golems, tools, etc. In addition, '
	db	'shouldst the desired object be one already imbued with the p'
	db	'ower of conversation, the Stone will enhance such powers, gi'
	db	'ving the object, or rather, creature, independent thought. H'
	db	'istorians claim that it is with this stone that Castambre co'
	db	'ncocted creatures of such deep personalities that, from behi'
	db	'nd a curtain, it was impossible to differentiated between a '
	db	'person and one of his creations.*'
	db	00
L8E03:	db	'     But how do I capture this ability, I hear thee ask.  Fi'
	db	'rst, assuming thou hast already discovered the Isle of Fire '
	db	'(no mean feat, I assure thee), thou must then search for the'
	db	' "pentacle of rocks" -- five boulders arranged as though the'
	db	'y were vertices in a pentagram. In the center thou shouldst '
	db	'notice a sixth rock, from which grows a large, healthy tree '
	db	'-- the Tree of Life. This sixth rock is Castambre\'s Stone.'
	db	00
L8FA6:	db	'     However, finding the Stone is only half the battle, for'
	db	' now thou must perform magicks beyonds the abilities of norm'
	db	'al men. With a ... thou must..........'
	db	00
L9045:	db	'... Once the the "heart" has been placed within the "chest" '
	db	'of the creature, the ritual may begin. First, using perhaps '
	db	'the same pick, thou must strike the Tree hard enough to draw'
	db	' blood. Blood from a Tree, questions thee? Aye, I say, for t'
	db	'his Tree is one of life and energy -- collected from the nut'
	db	'rients of the Stone, and bleed it does. Some say thou wilt b'
	db	'e able to hear the shrieks of pain from Castambre\'s Stone, '
	db	'but that rumor is waning. Thou wilt need enough of the Tree'
	db	'\'s life force to fill a bucket.'
	db	00
L9243:	db	'     After the blood has been properly contained, it must be'
	db	' spilled in five spots about the body of the stone creature '
	db	'as if the creature were Castambre\'s Stone and the puddles o'
	db	'f blood the five rocks of the pentacle. In fact, it is neces'
	db	'sary to set down five such small rocks to mark the location '
	db	'upon which the blood must be spilled. Then must thou cast Va'
	db	's Flam Uus (see detachable page at end of volume), setting f'
	db	'ire to each of the puddles of blood. Following that must be '
	db	'chanted the sacred words gleaned from Castambre\'s journals '
	db	'(also on detachable page).'
	db	00
L9478:	db	'     Now that the creature is enchanted, of course, it will '
	db	'become necessary to instruct it, much as one educates a chil'
	db	'd. However, a stone golem will learn much more quickly...'
	db	00
L952A:	db	'~~ ~~THE DARK CORE OF EXODUS~~ ~~by Erethian*'
	db	00
L9558:	db	'    Exodus was a mixture of etherial being and magical mecha'
	db	'nism. Its living portion, or psyche, was comprised of its am'
	db	'bitions, desires, curiosity, in total, its personality. The '
	db	'subject matter of this tome, however, lies upon a part of it'
	db	's more physical manifestation.'
	db	00
L9667:	db	'    The Core was the receptacle of Exodus\' memories and men'
	db	'tal force. The psyche was almost like a parasite, feeding of'
	db	'f the world around and depositing what it gained within the '
	db	'Core. What was Exodus\' purpose? Who or what did it serve? T'
	db	'his is matter of which I write.'
	db	00
L9775:	db	'    Exodus served none other than...'
	db	00
L979A:	db	'~~(The text remains unfinished.)'
	db	00
L97BB:	db	'~~ ~~Ethical Hedonism~~ ~~by R. Allen G.*'
	db	00
L97E5:	db	'    This handbook details a non-religous religion in which p'
	db	'eople live for the joy of living and make it their responsib'
	db	'ility to keep the entire world out of disrepair.'
	db	00
L988E:	db	'~~ ~~CONVERTING MOONGATES TO THINE OWN USE~~ ~~by Erethian*'
	db	00
L98CA:	db	'    Moongates come in four known varieties: blue, a gate acr'
	db	'oss a land, red, a gate connecting worlds, black, a gate tha'
	db	't traverses dimensions, and the theoretical silver, time gat'
	db	'e.'
	db	00
L9981:	db	'    These gates, to a lesser or greater degree, owe their po'
	db	'wer to the force exerted by large bodies of matter, moving t'
	db	'hrough the space around us. Therefore, I postulate that if o'
	db	'ne can know what forces are being exerted, one can manipulat'
	db	'e the destination of any given moongate by changing these fo'
	db	'rces.'
	db	00
L9AB3:	db	'    The book goes on to describe certain methods of manipula'
	db	'tion, and save for the prohibitively large amounts of power '
	db	'required to effect even the weakest of moongates, the logic '
	db	'seems sound...'
	db	00
